<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.7.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01856</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.7.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7bcacfe0-bca5-486b-bbbf-4ebeefcbdd36</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="d6ecaf8b-a27d-47f2-950b-bf6cbc2c53a0"><problem><para>
      <para>Use the following scenario to answer questions 1 – 5.</para>
      <blockquote>
        <para>Based on past musical productions, a theater predicts selling \(400-8p\) tickets when each ticket is sold at \(p\) dollars.</para>
      </blockquote>
      <para>Answer questions a–g to complete the middle column in the table to find out how many tickets the theater expects to sell and what revenues it expects to receive at the given ticket prices.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
        <thead>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>Ticket price (dollars)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Number of tickets sold</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Revenue (dollars)</emphasis></entry></row>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(5\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(360\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(1800\)</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(10\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>a. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(15\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>b. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(20\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>c. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(30\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>d. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(45\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>e. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(50\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>f. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(p\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>g. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      
      <para>When the price is \($10\) per ticket, what is the number of tickets sold? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>320</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 320. There are 320 tickets sold when the price is $10 per ticket. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
